    "concrete_cross_question_checks": [
      "first-five-double has the correct list 6,12,24,48,96. The positive branch of separated-terms-two-signs-positive uses the same values beginning at a_3, so selecting the correct first-five option can supply the positive tail 48,96 after an index shift. This overlap originates in the source numbers. It cannot supply the negative branch (-2,-48,96), and cannot justify selecting all possibilities without even-gap/sign reasoning.",
      "The exact tuple (q=2,a_6=48,a_7=96) appears as a true possibility in separated-terms-two-signs-positive and as option A in separated-terms-odd-gap. Copying it to the odd-gap item fails a_8=3 because it gives a_8=192. The odd-gap item still requires checking all its data.",
      "ratio-from-two-terms presents both-sign q^2 recovery and adjacent-term ratio claims as alternatives, not certified facts. Even knowing the correct symbolic results supplies a method for the 274 items, not their numeric q/a_6/a_7 sets or the opposite-sign impossibility.",
      "ratio-fourth-forward and ratio-fourth-reverse reverse the same 3,15 data. Their q values are reciprocals and their fourth terms are different. Repeated distractor tuple (5,1875) is not an unconditional answer; q=5 is compatible with only the forward order, and 1875 is then the fifth, not fourth, term.",
      "first-five-root-two and ratio-fourth-root-two share q=sqrt(2). Mapping the former's second and third entries to 2*sqrt(2),4 requires multiplying by 5 and shifting indices. The ratio-fourth item still distinguishes the third term 4*sqrt(2) from the fourth 8.",
      "The two prefix-recovery items share a backward method but not values or signs; the second needs q=-3/5 rather than 3/2. An earlier prefix tuple is not a later answer.",
      "The two two-sign separated-term items have the same option layout but distinct numeric equations q^2=4 and q^2=9 and different sign behavior for a_6. This is a repeated format, not an asserted common answer.",
      "recurrence-classification and examples-and-monotonicity happen to have independently true sets A-D. This answer-position pattern is a design cue worth noting, but no learner-facing text guarantees a prefix of true options, and borrowing that pattern supplies no proof of classification or monotonicity.",
      "domain-quadratic-poles requires retaining 7/4, and domain-two-holes-and-endpoint requires retaining 5. The general radical-zero rule transfers between them; the locations and denominator exclusions must still be recomputed."
    ],
    "quantifier_and_condition_checks": [
      "All six multiple stems explicitly request every true statement or every possible result. The two-sign items cannot be treated as single-choice selections.",
      "ratio-from-two-terms quantifies over real geometric sequences with positive integer k. Definition-implied nonzero terms license the divisions. E's universal inverse-ratio claim fails except for special q=+1 or -1; F's arbitrary-nonzero-pair claim fails for opposite signs.",
      "The real restriction in 274 is explicit, so q^2=-1 is an impossibility rather than permission to use imaginary q. The odd gap q^3=1/8 does not allow a negative branch.",
      "The positive-integer n in recurrence-classification and examples-and-monotonicity fixes the first term and the monotonicity range. Unary negatives in -2^(n-1) and -3^(n-1) negate the whole positive power, matching b_1<0.",
